WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.420
Here is the central puzzle of digestion.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.440
Your digestive enzymes are powerful enough to tear proteins apart, yet the cells

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.620
that make them are themselves built from protein.

4
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.840
So how do you manufacture a molecular shredder without shredding the factory?

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.220
The answer is the zymogen, an inactive precursor.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.900
The cell builds the enzyme in a folded-but-dormant form, ships it out, and normally

7
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.800
switches it on where digestion should happen.

8
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:33.640
This single design principle, activation only where and when needed, recurs

9
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.880
everywhere in physiology from blood clotting to apoptosis.

10
00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.900
Keep it in mind as our organizing idea.

11
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.050
Notice that digestion is really one chemical trick repeated three times.

12
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.490
Hydrolysis means breaking a bond by inserting water.

13
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:54.250
Proteases attack the peptide bond, disaccharidases attack the glycosidic bond, and

14
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.870
lipases attack the ester bond.

15
00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.250
In every case, water donates a hydrogen to one fragment and a hydroxyl to the other,

16
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.350
capping both freshly broken ends.

17
00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:07.990
The substrates look different, the enzymes are different, but the logic is

18
00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.130
identical. If you understand one row, you understand all three.

19
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.550
That bottom summary is the takeaway worth memorizing.

20
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.230
Water provides the H and the OH that finish off each broken bond.

21
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:24.183
This is the activation map for the digestive proteases.

22
00:01:24.923 --> 00:01:28.643
In the stomach, low pH converts pepsinogen into active pepsin.

23
00:01:29.163 --> 00:01:32.743
In the small intestine, the pancreas delivers a whole family of zymogens.

24
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:35.523
Watch what trypsin does once it is switched on.

25
00:01:35.903 --> 00:01:39.943
It activates chymotrypsinogen, the procarboxypeptidases, and proelastase.

26
00:01:40.723 --> 00:01:42.083
Trypsin is the master switch.

27
00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:47.483
That single arrow pattern, one activated enzyme turning on all the others, is why

28
00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:48.683
the note at the bottom matters.

29
00:01:49.423 --> 00:01:51.483
It also previews a clinical point.

30
00:01:52.263 --> 00:01:57.283
Knock out the ability to make trypsin and the entire downstream cascade never fires,

31
00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:00.103
no matter how much of the other zymogens you have.

32
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.027
Put the cascade idea to the test.

33
00:02:03.587 --> 00:02:05.787
On the left is the normal activation tree.

34
00:02:06.507 --> 00:02:11.027
Enteropeptidase switches on trypsin, and trypsin then activates chymotrypsin, the

35
00:02:11.027 --> 00:02:16.647
carboxypeptidases, elastase, and procolipase, a lipase helper that works even uncut.

36
00:02:17.227 --> 00:02:19.767
On the right, remove the ability to make trypsinogen.

37
00:02:20.487 --> 00:02:25.707
Now the master switch is gone, and the downstream protease zymogens stay locked, no

38
00:02:25.707 --> 00:02:27.127
matter how abundant they are.

39
00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:32.547
That is why a trypsinogen deficiency is so much worse than losing any single other

40
00:02:32.547 --> 00:02:36.047
zymogen. Lose one branch and you lose one enzyme.

41
00:02:36.907 --> 00:02:41.727
Lose trypsin, and pancreatic protein digestion collapses at once, while fat

42
00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.287
digestion is largely spared.
